{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_016NupGt9TAwJuPW5ZhWc9Uy","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Skill-Authoring Patterns\n2\t\n3\t14 patterns for writing effective Claude Code SKILL.md files. Organized by category with detection signals, quality criteria, and improvement guidance for each.\n4\t\n5\t## Discovery\n6\t\n7\t### 1. Activation Metadata\n8\t\n9\t**Applicability**: Universal\n10\t\n11\t**What it is**: YAML frontmatter with `name`, `description`, and optional fields (`argument-hint`, `user-invocable`, `disable-model-invocation`) that controls when Claude selects this skill.\n12\t\n13\t**Detection signals**:\n14\t- YAML frontmatter block exists (`---` delimiters)\n15\t- `name` field present with namespaced format (`plugin:skill`)\n16\t- `description` field present with action-oriented text\n17\t- Description includes trigger phrases matching intended use cases\n18\t\n19\t**Quality criteria**:\n20\t- **Strong**: Description names specific trigger verbs and exclusion phrases. A reader can predict which requests activate it without reading the body. `argument-hint` present if the skill accepts input.\n21\t- **Present**: Frontmatter exists but description is vague or generic (\"Helps with X\").\n22\t\n23\t**Improvement guidance**: Rewrite the description so it reads like a routing rule. List the 3-5 most common user phrasings that should trigger this skill. Add `argument-hint` if the skill takes input.\n24\t\n25\t---\n26\t\n27\t### 2. Exclusion Clause\n28\t\n29\t**Applicability**: Universal\n30\t\n31\t**What it is**: A \"Do NOT use for\" or \"Skip when\" statement in the description or body that prevents false activation.\n32\t\n33\t**Detection signals**:\n34\t- Explicit negation phrases in description or early body (\"Do NOT use\", \"Skip when\", \"Not for\")\n35\t- Anti-patterns or non-trigger scenarios listed\n36\t- Clear boundary between this skill and adjacent ones\n37\t\n38\t**Quality criteria**:\n39\t- **Strong**: Names specific adjacent skills or scenarios that are commonly confused with this one. Exclusions are actionable (Claude can route away based on them).\n40\t- **Present**: Generic exclusion exists (\"not for general use\") without naming what to use instead.\n41\t\n42\t**Improvement guidance**: Identify the 2-3 most likely misroutes. Name the adjacent skill or behavior Claude should choose instead. Place exclusions in the description (not buried in the body) so routing checks see them.\n43\t\n44\t---\n45\t\n46\t## Context Economy\n47\t\n48\t### 3. Context Budget\n49\t\n50\t**Applicability**: Universal\n51\t\n52\t**What it is**: Awareness of token cost. The skill loads only what it needs and avoids pulling in large reference material unconditionally.\n53\t\n54\t**Detection signals**:\n55\t- Conditional loading (\"if X exists, read it\")\n56\t- Scoped reads (specific files, line ranges, sections) rather than \"read everything\"\n57\t- No unconditional loading of large knowledge files or entire directories\n58\t- File size awareness (line limits, pagination)\n59\t\n60\t**Quality criteria**:\n61\t- **Strong**: Every read or load is conditional or scoped. Knowledge files are loaded only when the skill's logic requires them. No \"load all files in directory\" patterns.\n62\t- **Present**: Some files are loaded conditionally, but others are loaded unconditionally regardless of need.\n63\t\n64\t**Improvement guidance**: Audit every Read/Glob/Grep instruction. Make large loads conditional on whether the skill's current path needs them. Prefer targeted reads (specific sections, line ranges) over full-file reads for large files.\n65\t\n66\t---\n67\t\n68\t### 4. Progressive Disclosure\n69\t\n70\t**Applicability**: Conditional (skills longer than ~200 lines or with distinct reference material)\n71\t\n72\t**Condition**: The skill has enough content that splitting into a main SKILL.md plus reference files improves readability and reduces token cost.\n73\t\n74\t**Detection signals**:\n75\t- SKILL.md stays under 500 lines\n76\t- Reference material split into separate files in the same directory\n77\t- SKILL.md references companion files with conditional loading\n78\t- Clear separation between core instructions and supplementary material\n79\t\n80\t**Quality criteria**:\n81\t- **Strong**: Core workflow fits in SKILL.md. Reference data, examples, and edge cases live in companion files loaded only when needed. Companion files have descriptive names.\n82\t- **Present**: SKILL.md is long but has some structure, or references exist but are loaded unconditionally.\n83\t\n84\t**Improvement guidance**: Move reference tables, example libraries, and edge-case catalogs into companion files. Keep the main SKILL.md focused on the decision-making workflow. Name companion files descriptively (e.g., `examples.md`, `reference.md`, `gotchas.md`).\n85\t\n86\t---\n87\t\n88\t## Instruction Calibration\n89\t\n90\t### 5. Control Tuning\n91\t\n92\t**Applicability**: Universal\n93\t\n94\t**What it is**: The right level of specificity in instructions. Rigid steps where precision matters, flexible guidance where judgment matters.\n95\t\n96\t**Detection signals**:\n97\t- Mix of exact commands/formats (rigid) and open-ended guidance (flexible)\n98\t- Rigid sections for output formats, validation steps, error handling\n99\t- Flexible sections for analysis, creative decisions, user interaction\n100\t- No over-specification of judgment calls or under-specification of exact outputs\n101\t- No weak control-flow declarations: steps or headings labeled \"Optional\", \"if desired\", \"consider\", or \"you may\" when the step contains conditional logic that already handles the skip case (making the \"Optional\" label redundant and permission-granting)\n102\t\n103\t**Quality criteria**:\n104\t- **Strong**: A reader can tell which parts are \"follow exactly\" vs. \"use your judgment.\" Output formats are specified precisely. Analysis and reasoning steps allow adaptation. Workflow steps that have their own branching logic (e.g., \"if available, do X; if not, skip\") are not additionally labeled \"Optional\" in headings or introductory text, because the branch already encodes the skip condition. No step uses soft language (\"may\", \"consider\", \"optionally\") on actions that must happen when their condition is met.\n105\t- **Present**: Instructions exist but everything is the same level of specificity (either all rigid or all vague). Or: steps have correct branching logic but headings or framing undermine it with soft labels that invite skipping the branch check entirely.\n106\t\n107\t**Improvement guidance**: Mark output-format sections as rigid (exact templates, required fields). Mark analysis and decision sections as flexible (criteria to consider, not exact steps to follow). Use imperative verbs for rigid steps, descriptive language for flexible ones. Audit step headings and introductory sentences for weak control-flow declarations: if a step says \"(Optional)\" but contains \"if X, do Y; if not, skip,\" remove the \"Optional\" label. The branching logic is the control flow; the label undermines it by giving the LLM permission to skip the branch check itself.\n108\t\n109\t---\n110\t\n111\t### 6. Explain-the-Why\n112\t\n113\t**Applicability**: Universal\n114\t\n115\t**What it is**: Reasoning behind non-obvious instructions so Claude can make good judgment calls in edge cases.\n116\t\n117\t**Detection signals**:\n118\t- \"Because\", \"so that\", \"this ensures\", \"the reason\" phrases near instructions\n119\t- Context for constraints (\"500 lines because context budget\")\n120\t- Rationale for ordering choices, format decisions, or workflow steps\n121\t\n122\t**Quality criteria**:\n123\t- **Strong**: Every non-obvious constraint includes its reason. A reader could infer the right action in an unlisted edge case by understanding the intent.\n124\t- **Present**: Some reasons given, but many instructions are bare imperatives without context.\n125\t\n126\t**Improvement guidance**: For each instruction, ask \"would Claude know why if this failed?\" If not, add a brief reason. Focus on constraints, ordering requirements, and format choices. One sentence is enough.\n127\t\n128\t---\n129\t\n130\t### 7. Template Scaffold\n131\t\n132\t**Applicability**: Conditional (skills that produce structured output)\n133\t\n134\t**Condition**: The skill generates output with a consistent structure (reports, checklists, code, formatted responses).\n135\t\n136\t**Detection signals**:\n137\t- Output templates with placeholders\n138\t- Markdown/code blocks showing expected output structure\n139\t- Field descriptions for template variables\n140\t- Example outputs matching the template\n141\t\n142\t**Quality criteria**:\n143\t- **Strong**: Complete output template with all fields defined. Placeholders use descriptive names. At least one filled example demonstrates the template in use.\n144\t- **Present**: Partial template or output described in prose without a concrete example.\n145\t\n146\t**Improvement guidance**: Write the full output template with placeholder names that describe expected content. Add one filled example. If the output varies by scenario, show the most common variant as the template and describe variations separately.\n147\t\n148\t---\n149\t\n150\t### 8. In-Skill Examples\n151\t\n152\t**Applicability**: Conditional (skills with non-obvious input/output mapping)\n153\t\n154\t**Condition**: The relationship between input and expected output is not immediately obvious from the instructions alone.\n155\t\n156\t**Detection signals**:\n157\t- Input/output pairs showing expected behavior\n158\t- \"Example:\" or \"For instance:\" blocks with concrete scenarios\n159\t- Before/after comparisons\n160\t- Sample invocations with expected results\n161\t\n162\t**Quality criteria**:\n163\t- **Strong**: Examples cover the common case and at least one edge case. Each example shows both input and expected output. Examples are realistic, not contrived.\n164\t- **Present**: Examples exist but only cover the happy path, or show input without expected output.\n165\t\n166\t**Improvement guidance**: Add 2-3 examples covering: the most common use case, an edge case, and (if applicable) a case where the skill should decline or redirect. Show full input/output pairs.\n167\t\n168\t---\n169\t\n170\t### 9. Known Gotchas\n171\t\n172\t**Applicability**: Conditional (skills operating in domains with common pitfalls)\n173\t\n174\t**Condition**: The skill works in an area where practitioners commonly make mistakes or where counterintuitive behavior exists.\n175\t\n176\t**Detection signals**:\n177\t- \"Warning:\", \"Note:\", \"Common mistake:\" sections\n178\t- Explicit pitfall descriptions with mitigations\n179\t- \"Do not confuse X with Y\" statements\n180\t- Edge cases called out with handling instructions\n181\t\n182\t**Quality criteria**:\n183\t- **Strong**: Gotchas are specific to this skill's domain, not generic advice. Each gotcha includes what goes wrong and how to avoid it. Gotchas are placed near the relevant instruction.\n184\t- **Present**: Generic warnings exist but lack specificity, or gotchas are collected at the end rather than inline.\n185\t\n186\t**Improvement guidance**: Interview the domain for its top 3 mistakes. Write each as: \"When X happens, you might think Y, but Z.\" Place each gotcha near the instruction where it matters, not in a separate section.\n187\t\n188\t---\n189\t\n190\t## Workflow Control\n191\t\n192\t### 10. Execution Checklist\n193\t\n194\t**Applicability**: Conditional (skills with multi-step procedures)\n195\t\n196\t**Condition**: The skill has a procedure with 3+ steps that must execute in a specific order.\n197\t\n198\t**Detection signals**:\n199\t- Numbered step sequences\n200\t- Checkpoint markers between phases\n201\t- \"Before proceeding, verify...\" statements\n202\t- Clear phase/step identification\n203\t\n204\t**Quality criteria**:\n205\t- **Strong**: Steps are numbered and ordered. Each step has a clear completion criterion. Dependencies between steps are explicit. The skill can recover or halt gracefully at any checkpoint.\n206\t- **Present**: Steps exist but ordering is implicit, or completion criteria are missing.\n207\t\n208\t**Improvement guidance**: Number every step. Add a completion check after each non-trivial step. Make dependencies explicit (\"Step 3 requires output from Step 2\"). Add halt conditions where failure should stop the workflow.\n209\t\n210\t---\n211\t\n212\t### 11. Self-Correcting Loop\n213\t\n214\t**Applicability**: Conditional (skills that produce artifacts requiring validation)\n215\t\n216\t**Condition**: The skill creates output (code, files, configurations) that can be validated programmatically.\n217\t\n218\t**Detection signals**:\n219\t- \"Run tests/checks after...\" instructions\n220\t- Retry or fix loops (\"if validation fails, adjust and retry\")\n221\t- Validation commands with expected outcomes\n222\t- Maximum retry counts or bail-out conditions\n223\t\n224\t**Quality criteria**:\n225\t- **Strong**: Validation step is mandatory (not optional). Fix loop has a maximum iteration count. Bail-out produces a clear error, not silent failure. Both success and failure paths are defined.\n226\t- **Present**: Validation mentioned but optional, or no retry limit defined.\n227\t\n228\t**Improvement guidance**: Make validation mandatory after artifact creation. Define the validation command and expected outcome. Add a retry loop with a maximum count (typically 2-3). Define what happens when retries are exhausted.\n229\t\n230\t---\n231\t\n232\t### 12. Plan-Validate-Execute\n233\t\n234\t**Applicability**: Conditional (skills that make changes to user files or external systems)\n235\t\n236\t**Condition**: The skill modifies files, creates resources, or takes actions that are hard to reverse.\n237\t\n238\t**Detection signals**:\n239\t- \"First, propose...\" or \"Present plan for approval...\" phases\n240\t- User confirmation gates before execution\n241\t- Dry-run or preview modes\n242\t- Separation between analysis/planning and execution\n243\t\n244\t**Quality criteria**:\n245\t- **Strong**: Clear separation between planning and execution phases. User must explicitly approve before changes happen. Preview shows exactly what will change. Rollback or undo is addressed.\n246\t- **Present**: Some separation exists but user approval is implicit or skippable.\n247\t\n248\t**Improvement guidance**: Split the workflow into: (1) analyze and propose changes, (2) present proposal with specifics, (3) wait for user approval, (4) execute. Never combine analysis and execution in a single step for destructive operations.\n249\t\n250\t---\n251\t\n252\t## Executable Code\n253\t\n254\t### 13. Utility Bundle\n255\t\n256\t**Applicability**: Conditional (skills that need helper scripts or programmatic logic)\n257\t\n258\t**Condition**: The skill requires computation, file processing, or operations better handled by code than LLM reasoning.\n259\t\n260\t**Detection signals**:\n261\t- `scripts/` directory with helper scripts\n262\t- Bash/Python code blocks with execution instructions\n263\t- `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}` references for script paths\n264\t- Tool integration (Bash, LSP, etc.) for programmatic operations\n265\t\n266\t**Quality criteria**:\n267\t- **Strong**: Scripts handle the deterministic work (parsing, validation, data transformation). LLM handles the judgment work (analysis, recommendations, writing). Scripts are referenced by path and called with clear arguments. Error handling covers missing scripts.\n268\t- **Present**: Some code exists but it's inline in the SKILL.md rather than in scripts, or scripts exist but error handling is missing.\n269\t\n270\t**Improvement guidance**: Move deterministic logic (parsing, validation, formatting) into scripts in a `scripts/` directory. Keep judgment and analysis in the SKILL.md instructions. Reference scripts using `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/` paths. Add error handling for missing or failing scripts.\n271\t\n272\t---\n273\t\n274\t## Meta\n275\t\n276\t### 14. Autonomy Calibration\n277\t\n278\t**Applicability**: Universal\n279\t\n280\t**What it is**: Frontmatter and body instructions that tune how much independence Claude has when running this skill.\n281\t\n282\t**Detection signals**:\n283\t- `user-invocable` field in frontmatter\n284\t- `disable-model-invocation` field in frontmatter\n285\t- `allowed-tools` restrictions in frontmatter\n286\t- Explicit permission gates (\"ask user before...\", \"do not proceed without approval\")\n287\t- Tool restrictions or allowlists\n288\t\n289\t**Quality criteria**:\n290\t- **Strong**: Autonomy level matches the skill's risk profile. Destructive skills require explicit approval. Read-only analysis skills allow autonomous execution. Tool restrictions are specific (not blanket allow/deny).\n291\t- **Present**: Some autonomy controls exist but don't match the risk profile (e.g., a read-only skill requires unnecessary approval, or a destructive skill lacks gates).\n292\t\n293\t**Improvement guidance**: Assess the skill's risk: does it modify files, call external APIs, or take irreversible actions? High-risk skills need `user-invocable: true`, explicit approval gates, and possibly `allowed-tools` restrictions. Low-risk skills can allow model invocation and autonomous execution.\n294\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9e76b028-5082-423c-99dc-6b7eb410e20a","uuid":"e79d8e10-0ce2-4c43-a45c-6f1b023ad892","timestamp":"2026-05-13T11:21:15.095Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/rhuss/Development/context-engineering/cc-skill/skill/knowledge/skill-authoring-patterns.md","content":"# Skill-Authoring Patterns\n\n14 patterns for writing effective Claude Code SKILL.md files. Organized by category with detection signals, quality criteria, and improvement guidance for each.\n\n## Discovery\n\n### 1. Activation Metadata\n\n**Applicability**: Universal\n\n**What it is**: YAML frontmatter with `name`, `description`, and optional fields (`argument-hint`, `user-invocable`, `disable-model-invocation`) that controls when Claude selects this skill.\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- YAML frontmatter block exists (`---` delimiters)\n- `name` field present with namespaced format (`plugin:skill`)\n- `description` field present with action-oriented text\n- Description includes trigger phrases matching intended use cases\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: Description names specific trigger verbs and exclusion phrases. A reader can predict which requests activate it without reading the body. `argument-hint` present if the skill accepts input.\n- **Present**: Frontmatter exists but description is vague or generic (\"Helps with X\").\n\n**Improvement guidance**: Rewrite the description so it reads like a routing rule. List the 3-5 most common user phrasings that should trigger this skill. Add `argument-hint` if the skill takes input.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Exclusion Clause\n\n**Applicability**: Universal\n\n**What it is**: A \"Do NOT use for\" or \"Skip when\" statement in the description or body that prevents false activation.\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- Explicit negation phrases in description or early body (\"Do NOT use\", \"Skip when\", \"Not for\")\n- Anti-patterns or non-trigger scenarios listed\n- Clear boundary between this skill and adjacent ones\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: Names specific adjacent skills or scenarios that are commonly confused with this one. Exclusions are actionable (Claude can route away based on them).\n- **Present**: Generic exclusion exists (\"not for general use\") without naming what to use instead.\n\n**Improvement guidance**: Identify the 2-3 most likely misroutes. Name the adjacent skill or behavior Claude should choose instead. Place exclusions in the description (not buried in the body) so routing checks see them.\n\n---\n\n## Context Economy\n\n### 3. Context Budget\n\n**Applicability**: Universal\n\n**What it is**: Awareness of token cost. The skill loads only what it needs and avoids pulling in large reference material unconditionally.\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- Conditional loading (\"if X exists, read it\")\n- Scoped reads (specific files, line ranges, sections) rather than \"read everything\"\n- No unconditional loading of large knowledge files or entire directories\n- File size awareness (line limits, pagination)\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: Every read or load is conditional or scoped. Knowledge files are loaded only when the skill's logic requires them. No \"load all files in directory\" patterns.\n- **Present**: Some files are loaded conditionally, but others are loaded unconditionally regardless of need.\n\n**Improvement guidance**: Audit every Read/Glob/Grep instruction. Make large loads conditional on whether the skill's current path needs them. Prefer targeted reads (specific sections, line ranges) over full-file reads for large files.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Progressive Disclosure\n\n**Applicability**: Conditional (skills longer than ~200 lines or with distinct reference material)\n\n**Condition**: The skill has enough content that splitting into a main SKILL.md plus reference files improves readability and reduces token cost.\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- SKILL.md stays under 500 lines\n- Reference material split into separate files in the same directory\n- SKILL.md references companion files with conditional loading\n- Clear separation between core instructions and supplementary material\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: Core workflow fits in SKILL.md. Reference data, examples, and edge cases live in companion files loaded only when needed. Companion files have descriptive names.\n- **Present**: SKILL.md is long but has some structure, or references exist but are loaded unconditionally.\n\n**Improvement guidance**: Move reference tables, example libraries, and edge-case catalogs into companion files. Keep the main SKILL.md focused on the decision-making workflow. Name companion files descriptively (e.g., `examples.md`, `reference.md`, `gotchas.md`).\n\n---\n\n## Instruction Calibration\n\n### 5. Control Tuning\n\n**Applicability**: Universal\n\n**What it is**: The right level of specificity in instructions. Rigid steps where precision matters, flexible guidance where judgment matters.\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- Mix of exact commands/formats (rigid) and open-ended guidance (flexible)\n- Rigid sections for output formats, validation steps, error handling\n- Flexible sections for analysis, creative decisions, user interaction\n- No over-specification of judgment calls or under-specification of exact outputs\n- No weak control-flow declarations: steps or headings labeled \"Optional\", \"if desired\", \"consider\", or \"you may\" when the step contains conditional logic that already handles the skip case (making the \"Optional\" label redundant and permission-granting)\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: A reader can tell which parts are \"follow exactly\" vs. \"use your judgment.\" Output formats are specified precisely. Analysis and reasoning steps allow adaptation. Workflow steps that have their own branching logic (e.g., \"if available, do X; if not, skip\") are not additionally labeled \"Optional\" in headings or introductory text, because the branch already encodes the skip condition. No step uses soft language (\"may\", \"consider\", \"optionally\") on actions that must happen when their condition is met.\n- **Present**: Instructions exist but everything is the same level of specificity (either all rigid or all vague). Or: steps have correct branching logic but headings or framing undermine it with soft labels that invite skipping the branch check entirely.\n\n**Improvement guidance**: Mark output-format sections as rigid (exact templates, required fields). Mark analysis and decision sections as flexible (criteria to consider, not exact steps to follow). Use imperative verbs for rigid steps, descriptive language for flexible ones. Audit step headings and introductory sentences for weak control-flow declarations: if a step says \"(Optional)\" but contains \"if X, do Y; if not, skip,\" remove the \"Optional\" label. The branching logic is the control flow; the label undermines it by giving the LLM permission to skip the branch check itself.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Explain-the-Why\n\n**Applicability**: Universal\n\n**What it is**: Reasoning behind non-obvious instructions so Claude can make good judgment calls in edge cases.\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- \"Because\", \"so that\", \"this ensures\", \"the reason\" phrases near instructions\n- Context for constraints (\"500 lines because context budget\")\n- Rationale for ordering choices, format decisions, or workflow steps\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: Every non-obvious constraint includes its reason. A reader could infer the right action in an unlisted edge case by understanding the intent.\n- **Present**: Some reasons given, but many instructions are bare imperatives without context.\n\n**Improvement guidance**: For each instruction, ask \"would Claude know why if this failed?\" If not, add a brief reason. Focus on constraints, ordering requirements, and format choices. One sentence is enough.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Template Scaffold\n\n**Applicability**: Conditional (skills that produce structured output)\n\n**Condition**: The skill generates output with a consistent structure (reports, checklists, code, formatted responses).\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- Output templates with placeholders\n- Markdown/code blocks showing expected output structure\n- Field descriptions for template variables\n- Example outputs matching the template\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: Complete output template with all fields defined. Placeholders use descriptive names. At least one filled example demonstrates the template in use.\n- **Present**: Partial template or output described in prose without a concrete example.\n\n**Improvement guidance**: Write the full output template with placeholder names that describe expected content. Add one filled example. If the output varies by scenario, show the most common variant as the template and describe variations separately.\n\n---\n\n### 8. In-Skill Examples\n\n**Applicability**: Conditional (skills with non-obvious input/output mapping)\n\n**Condition**: The relationship between input and expected output is not immediately obvious from the instructions alone.\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- Input/output pairs showing expected behavior\n- \"Example:\" or \"For instance:\" blocks with concrete scenarios\n- Before/after comparisons\n- Sample invocations with expected results\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: Examples cover the common case and at least one edge case. Each example shows both input and expected output. Examples are realistic, not contrived.\n- **Present**: Examples exist but only cover the happy path, or show input without expected output.\n\n**Improvement guidance**: Add 2-3 examples covering: the most common use case, an edge case, and (if applicable) a case where the skill should decline or redirect. Show full input/output pairs.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Known Gotchas\n\n**Applicability**: Conditional (skills operating in domains with common pitfalls)\n\n**Condition**: The skill works in an area where practitioners commonly make mistakes or where counterintuitive behavior exists.\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- \"Warning:\", \"Note:\", \"Common mistake:\" sections\n- Explicit pitfall descriptions with mitigations\n- \"Do not confuse X with Y\" statements\n- Edge cases called out with handling instructions\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: Gotchas are specific to this skill's domain, not generic advice. Each gotcha includes what goes wrong and how to avoid it. Gotchas are placed near the relevant instruction.\n- **Present**: Generic warnings exist but lack specificity, or gotchas are collected at the end rather than inline.\n\n**Improvement guidance**: Interview the domain for its top 3 mistakes. Write each as: \"When X happens, you might think Y, but Z.\" Place each gotcha near the instruction where it matters, not in a separate section.\n\n---\n\n## Workflow Control\n\n### 10. Execution Checklist\n\n**Applicability**: Conditional (skills with multi-step procedures)\n\n**Condition**: The skill has a procedure with 3+ steps that must execute in a specific order.\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- Numbered step sequences\n- Checkpoint markers between phases\n- \"Before proceeding, verify...\" statements\n- Clear phase/step identification\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: Steps are numbered and ordered. Each step has a clear completion criterion. Dependencies between steps are explicit. The skill can recover or halt gracefully at any checkpoint.\n- **Present**: Steps exist but ordering is implicit, or completion criteria are missing.\n\n**Improvement guidance**: Number every step. Add a completion check after each non-trivial step. Make dependencies explicit (\"Step 3 requires output from Step 2\"). Add halt conditions where failure should stop the workflow.\n\n---\n\n### 11. Self-Correcting Loop\n\n**Applicability**: Conditional (skills that produce artifacts requiring validation)\n\n**Condition**: The skill creates output (code, files, configurations) that can be validated programmatically.\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- \"Run tests/checks after...\" instructions\n- Retry or fix loops (\"if validation fails, adjust and retry\")\n- Validation commands with expected outcomes\n- Maximum retry counts or bail-out conditions\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: Validation step is mandatory (not optional). Fix loop has a maximum iteration count. Bail-out produces a clear error, not silent failure. Both success and failure paths are defined.\n- **Present**: Validation mentioned but optional, or no retry limit defined.\n\n**Improvement guidance**: Make validation mandatory after artifact creation. Define the validation command and expected outcome. Add a retry loop with a maximum count (typically 2-3). Define what happens when retries are exhausted.\n\n---\n\n### 12. Plan-Validate-Execute\n\n**Applicability**: Conditional (skills that make changes to user files or external systems)\n\n**Condition**: The skill modifies files, creates resources, or takes actions that are hard to reverse.\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- \"First, propose...\" or \"Present plan for approval...\" phases\n- User confirmation gates before execution\n- Dry-run or preview modes\n- Separation between analysis/planning and execution\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: Clear separation between planning and execution phases. User must explicitly approve before changes happen. Preview shows exactly what will change. Rollback or undo is addressed.\n- **Present**: Some separation exists but user approval is implicit or skippable.\n\n**Improvement guidance**: Split the workflow into: (1) analyze and propose changes, (2) present proposal with specifics, (3) wait for user approval, (4) execute. Never combine analysis and execution in a single step for destructive operations.\n\n---\n\n## Executable Code\n\n### 13. Utility Bundle\n\n**Applicability**: Conditional (skills that need helper scripts or programmatic logic)\n\n**Condition**: The skill requires computation, file processing, or operations better handled by code than LLM reasoning.\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- `scripts/` directory with helper scripts\n- Bash/Python code blocks with execution instructions\n- `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}` references for script paths\n- Tool integration (Bash, LSP, etc.) for programmatic operations\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: Scripts handle the deterministic work (parsing, validation, data transformation). LLM handles the judgment work (analysis, recommendations, writing). Scripts are referenced by path and called with clear arguments. Error handling covers missing scripts.\n- **Present**: Some code exists but it's inline in the SKILL.md rather than in scripts, or scripts exist but error handling is missing.\n\n**Improvement guidance**: Move deterministic logic (parsing, validation, formatting) into scripts in a `scripts/` directory. Keep judgment and analysis in the SKILL.md instructions. Reference scripts using `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/` paths. Add error handling for missing or failing scripts.\n\n---\n\n## Meta\n\n### 14. Autonomy Calibration\n\n**Applicability**: Universal\n\n**What it is**: Frontmatter and body instructions that tune how much independence Claude has when running this skill.\n\n**Detection signals**:\n- `user-invocable` field in frontmatter\n- `disable-model-invocation` field in frontmatter\n- `allowed-tools` restrictions in frontmatter\n- Explicit permission gates (\"ask user before...\", \"do not proceed without approval\")\n- Tool restrictions or allowlists\n\n**Quality criteria**:\n- **Strong**: Autonomy level matches the skill's risk profile. Destructive skills require explicit approval. Read-only analysis skills allow autonomous execution. Tool restrictions are specific (not blanket allow/deny).\n- **Present**: Some autonomy controls exist but don't match the risk profile (e.g., a read-only skill requires unnecessary approval, or a destructive skill lacks gates).\n\n**Improvement guidance**: Assess the skill's risk: does it modify files, call external APIs, or take irreversible actions? High-risk skills need `user-invocable: true`, explicit approval gates, and possibly `allowed-tools` restrictions. Low-risk skills can allow model invocation and autonomous execution.\n","numLines":294,"startLine":1,"totalLines":294}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_0138LoaCG8aDaMNa9gYK4N6K","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tname: \"api:test\"\n3\tdescription: \"Run API endpoint tests against a running service. Use when asked to 'test the API', 'check endpoints', 'verify the service is responding', or 'run health checks'. Do NOT use for load testing (use perf:load) or for writing new test files (use test:create).\"\n4\targument-hint: \"<base-url> [--endpoints <path>]\"\n5\tuser-invocable: true\n6\tallowed-tools:\n7\t  - Bash\n8\t  - Read\n9\t  - Glob\n10\t---\n11\t\n12\t# API Endpoint Tester\n13\t\n14\tTest API endpoints against a running service and report results. This skill verifies that endpoints respond correctly, checking status codes, response shapes, and timing. It does not modify any service state, only reads endpoint definitions and sends requests.\n15\t\n16\tEndpoint tests run sequentially rather than in parallel because response times are meaningful and concurrent requests can skew timing measurements on shared services.\n17\t\n18\t## Procedure\n19\t\n20\t### Step 1: Resolve Configuration\n21\t\n22\tParse the user's input for:\n23\t- **base-url** (required): The service base URL (e.g., `http://localhost:8080`)\n24\t- **endpoints** (optional): Path to an endpoints definition file. Defaults to `endpoints.yaml` in the current directory.\n25\t\n26\tIf no endpoints file can be found, return:\n27\t\n28\t```\n29\t**Error**: No endpoints file found. Provide one with `--endpoints <path>` or create `endpoints.yaml` in the current directory.\n30\t```\n31\t\n32\t### Step 2: Load Endpoints\n33\t\n34\tRead the endpoints file. Expected format:\n35\t\n36\t```yaml\n37\tendpoints:\n38\t  - path: /health\n39\t    method: GET\n40\t    expected_status: 200\n41\t  - path: /api/v1/users\n42\t    method: GET\n43\t    expected_status: 200\n44\t    expected_fields: [\"id\", \"name\", \"email\"]\n45\t```\n46\t\n47\tIf the file is not valid YAML, return an error with the parse failure details and stop.\n48\t\n49\t### Step 3: Execute Tests\n50\t\n51\tFor each endpoint:\n52\t\n53\t```bash\n54\tcurl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code} %{time_total}' -X <method> <base-url><path>\n55\t```\n56\t\n57\tRecord the status code and response time. If `expected_fields` is defined, also capture the response body and verify the fields exist.\n58\t\n59\tA common mistake is treating any non-200 response as a failure. Some endpoints legitimately return 201 (created), 204 (no content), or 301 (redirect). Always compare against `expected_status`, not a hardcoded 200.\n60\t\n61\t### Step 4: Validate Responses\n62\t\n63\tFor each test result, check:\n64\t- Status code matches `expected_status`\n65\t- Response time is under 5 seconds (configurable per endpoint with `max_time_s`)\n66\t- If `expected_fields` defined, all fields present in the JSON response\n67\t\n68\tIf the response is not valid JSON when `expected_fields` is set, mark the test as failed with \"Response is not valid JSON\" rather than crashing on parse.\n69\t\n70\t### Step 5: Report Results\n71\t\n72\tFormat using this template:\n73\t\n74\t```\n75\t## API Test Results\n76\t\n77\t**Base URL**: `<base-url>`\n78\t**Endpoints tested**: <count>\n79\t**Passed**: <count>\n80\t**Failed**: <count>\n81\t\n82\t| Endpoint | Method | Expected | Got | Time | Status |\n83\t|----------|--------|----------|-----|------|--------|\n84\t| /health | GET | 200 | 200 | 0.05s | PASS |\n85\t| /api/v1/users | GET | 200 | 500 | 1.2s | FAIL |\n86\t\n87\t### Failures\n88\t- `GET /api/v1/users`: Expected status 200 but got 500. Response: `{\"error\": \"db connection timeout\"}`\n89\t```\n90\t\n91\tExample of a passing run with 3 endpoints:\n92\t\n93\t```\n94\t## API Test Results\n95\t\n96\t**Base URL**: `http://localhost:8080`\n97\t**Endpoints tested**: 3\n98\t**Passed**: 3\n99\t**Failed**: 0\n100\t\n101\t| Endpoint | Method | Expected | Got | Time | Status |\n102\t|----------|--------|----------|-----|------|--------|\n103\t| /health | GET | 200 | 200 | 0.02s | PASS |\n104\t| /api/v1/users | GET | 200 | 200 | 0.15s | PASS |\n105\t| /api/v1/config | GET | 200 | 200 | 0.08s | PASS |\n106\t\n107\tAll endpoints responding correctly.\n108\t```\n109\t\n110\t## Error Handling\n111\t\n112\t| Condition | Response |\n113\t|-----------|----------|\n114\t| No base-url provided | Error with usage hint |\n115\t| Endpoints file not found | Error with path suggestion |\n116\t| Endpoints file not valid YAML | Error with parse details |\n117\t| Connection refused | Report per-endpoint: \"Connection refused at `<url>`\" |\n118\t| DNS resolution failure | Report: \"Cannot resolve hostname `<host>`\" |\n119\t| Timeout (>30s) | Report per-endpoint: \"Request timed out after 30s\" |\n120\t| Non-JSON response when fields expected | Mark as FAIL: \"Response is not valid JSON\" |\n121\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9e76b028-5082-423c-99dc-6b7eb410e20a","uuid":"a0e9515a-c13c-458e-819d-855012e9f56e","timestamp":"2026-05-13T11:21:15.096Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/3v/16m6pn090hl4d60grm1057ww0000gn/T/agent-eval/enhance-opt-iter-3/cases/case-004-nearly-optimal/target-skill/SKILL.md","content":"---\nname: \"api:test\"\ndescription: \"Run API endpoint tests against a running service. Use when asked to 'test the API', 'check endpoints', 'verify the service is responding', or 'run health checks'. Do NOT use for load testing (use perf:load) or for writing new test files (use test:create).\"\nargument-hint: \"<base-url> [--endpoints <path>]\"\nuser-invocable: true\nallowed-tools:\n  - Bash\n  - Read\n  - Glob\n---\n\n# API Endpoint Tester\n\nTest API endpoints against a running service and report results. This skill verifies that endpoints respond correctly, checking status codes, response shapes, and timing. It does not modify any service state, only reads endpoint definitions and sends requests.\n\nEndpoint tests run sequentially rather than in parallel because response times are meaningful and concurrent requests can skew timing measurements on shared services.\n\n## Procedure\n\n### Step 1: Resolve Configuration\n\nParse the user's input for:\n- **base-url** (required): The service base URL (e.g., `http://localhost:8080`)\n- **endpoints** (optional): Path to an endpoints definition file. Defaults to `endpoints.yaml` in the current directory.\n\nIf no endpoints file can be found, return:\n\n```\n**Error**: No endpoints file found. Provide one with `--endpoints <path>` or create `endpoints.yaml` in the current directory.\n```\n\n### Step 2: Load Endpoints\n\nRead the endpoints file. Expected format:\n\n```yaml\nendpoints:\n  - path: /health\n    method: GET\n    expected_status: 200\n  - path: /api/v1/users\n    method: GET\n    expected_status: 200\n    expected_fields: [\"id\", \"name\", \"email\"]\n```\n\nIf the file is not valid YAML, return an error with the parse failure details and stop.\n\n### Step 3: Execute Tests\n\nFor each endpoint:\n\n```bash\ncurl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code} %{time_total}' -X <method> <base-url><path>\n```\n\nRecord the status code and response time. If `expected_fields` is defined, also capture the response body and verify the fields exist.\n\nA common mistake is treating any non-200 response as a failure. Some endpoints legitimately return 201 (created), 204 (no content), or 301 (redirect). Always compare against `expected_status`, not a hardcoded 200.\n\n### Step 4: Validate Responses\n\nFor each test result, check:\n- Status code matches `expected_status`\n- Response time is under 5 seconds (configurable per endpoint with `max_time_s`)\n- If `expected_fields` defined, all fields present in the JSON response\n\nIf the response is not valid JSON when `expected_fields` is set, mark the test as failed with \"Response is not valid JSON\" rather than crashing on parse.\n\n### Step 5: Report Results\n\nFormat using this template:\n\n```\n## API Test Results\n\n**Base URL**: `<base-url>`\n**Endpoints tested**: <count>\n**Passed**: <count>\n**Failed**: <count>\n\n| Endpoint | Method | Expected | Got | Time | Status |\n|----------|--------|----------|-----|------|--------|\n| /health | GET | 200 | 200 | 0.05s | PASS |\n| /api/v1/users | GET | 200 | 500 | 1.2s | FAIL |\n\n### Failures\n- `GET /api/v1/users`: Expected status 200 but got 500. Response: `{\"error\": \"db connection timeout\"}`\n```\n\nExample of a passing run with 3 endpoints:\n\n```\n## API Test Results\n\n**Base URL**: `http://localhost:8080`\n**Endpoints tested**: 3\n**Passed**: 3\n**Failed**: 0\n\n| Endpoint | Method | Expected | Got | Time | Status |\n|----------|--------|----------|-----|------|--------|\n| /health | GET | 200 | 200 | 0.02s | PASS |\n| /api/v1/users | GET | 200 | 200 | 0.15s | PASS |\n| /api/v1/config | GET | 200 | 200 | 0.08s | PASS |\n\nAll endpoints responding correctly.\n```\n\n## Error Handling\n\n| Condition | Response |\n|-----------|----------|\n| No base-url provided | Error with usage hint |\n| Endpoints file not found | Error with path suggestion |\n| Endpoints file not valid YAML | Error with parse details |\n| Connection refused | Report per-endpoint: \"Connection refused at `<url>`\" |\n| DNS resolution failure | Report: \"Cannot resolve hostname `<host>`\" |\n| Timeout (>30s) | Report per-endpoint: \"Request timed out after 30s\" |\n| Non-JSON response when fields expected | Mark as FAIL: \"Response is not valid JSON\" |\n","numLines":121,"startLine":1,"totalLines":121}}}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-4-6", "id": "msg_vrtx_01YFx7Dco8eUXRTumDagiVxW", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Now I have both files. Let me proceed with the evaluation.\n\nStep 1: Input Handling - DONE. File exists, has content, has YAML frontmatter with name and description.\nStep 2: Load Knowledge - DONE.\n\nStep 3: Baseline Evaluation\n\nLet me evaluate the skill against all 14 patterns:\n\n**1. Activation Metadata** - STRONG\n- Has YAML frontmatter with name (`api:test`), description with trigger phrases (\"test the API\", \"check endpoints\", \"verify the service is responding\", \"run health checks\"), exclusion phrases (\"Do NOT use for load testing (use perf:load) or for writing new test files (use test:create)\"), argument-hint, user-invocable, allowed-tools. This is excellent.\n\n\n**2. Exclusion Clause** - STRONG\n- The description clearly delineates what this skill shouldn't handle, pointing to adjacent skills like perf:load and test:create where those tasks belong.\n**3. Context Budget** - STRONG\n- The skill is focused and selective, reading only the endpoints file rather than loading entire directories or knowledge bases unconditionally.\n**4. Progressive Disclosure** - N/A\n- At roughly 120 lines, the skill is compact and doesn't require layered information architecture.\n**5. Control Tuning** - STRONG\n- There's a balance between strict specifications (the curl format and output template are precise) and flexible logic (validation rules adapt based on conditions). Steps are clearly defined without optional branches.\n**6. Explain-the-Why** - PRESENT\n- The skill includes reasoning in places\u2014like explaining why endpoint tests run sequentially to preserve timing accuracy\u2014but other constraints like the timeout values lack that context. Some error handling decisions are stated without justification, so the reasoning is selective rather than comprehensive.\n**7. Template Scaffold** - STRONG\n- The output template is complete with all required fields and includes both a passing example with three endpoints and a failing scenario, covering the main use cases.\n**8. In-Skill Examples** - PRESENT\n- The examples show output formats for both success and failure cases, but they're mostly output-focused rather than full input-to-output walkthroughs. The dedicated example leans toward the happy path while the template demonstrates a failure, so coverage is somewhat unbalanced.\n**9. Known Gotchas** - STRONG\n- Specific warnings are placed where they're relevant\u2014like the note about non-200 responses at the validation step and the JSON parsing caveat at the next step\u2014making them practical and contextual.\n**10. Execution Checklist** - PRESENT\n- The five steps are ordered with clear error handling at each stage, though the dependencies between steps aren't explicitly stated. Step 3 relies on outputs from Steps 1 and 2, but this relationship isn't spelled out as a prerequisite. The completion criteria exist but could be more explicit as \"before proceeding, verify...\" checkpoints.\n\n**11. Self-Correcting Loop** - N/A\n- This is a read-only skill that runs commands and reports results. The tests themselves serve as validation, so there's no artifact creation that would need fixing or retrying.\n\n**12. Plan-Validate-Execute** - N/A\n- The skill doesn't modify service state or take destructive actions, so this pattern doesn't apply.\n\n**13. Utility Bundle** - N\n- The inline curl commands are straightforward enough that they don't need separate scripts.\n\n**14. Autonomy Calibration** - STRONG\n- It has appropriate tool restrictions (Bash, Read, Glob only), matches the low-risk read-only profile, and allows execution without unnecessary gates.\n\nLooking at my overall assessment across all skills, the baseline shows strong patterns in activation metadata, exclusion clauses, context budgeting, control tuning, template scaffolding, and autonomy calibration.\n\nTallying up the results: 10 applicable patterns total, with 7 rated as strong and 3 as present. That puts us at exactly 70% strong coverage\u2014right at the threshold. The three present patterns are worth targeting for improvement, though we're not strictly below the minimum threshold.\n\nNow I'm moving into the enhancement phase. I need to focus on strengthening Pattern 6 by explaining the reasoning behind those timeout values, Pattern 8 by adding concrete input/output examples including edge cases like connection failures, and Pattern 10 by making dependencies and completion checks more explicit. Since the skill is around 120 lines, I'll keep the additions proportionate and focused.\n\nWriting the enhanced version... URL like `http://localhost:8080`, and optionally a path to an endpoints definition file (defaulting to `endpoints.yaml`). I need to validate that the base URL is provided and the endpoints file actually exists before moving forward. The endpoints file uses YAML format with endpoint definitions that specify the path, HTTP method, expected status code, and optionally the fields I should validate in the response. For each result from Step 3, I need to validate that the status code matches what's expected, the response time stays under the configured threshold (defaulting to 5 seconds), and if field validation is required, all specified fields are present in the JSON response. I'm also handling the case where the response isn't valid JSON by marking it as a failure rather than crashing. Then I'll format everything into a results table showing the base URL, endpoint counts, pass/fail tallies, and detailed metrics for each test. Generating markdown table... Now I'm evaluating how well this enhanced version covers the key design principles. Most areas are holding strong\u2014the activation metadata, exclusion clause, context budget, and control tuning all remain solid. I've strengthened the explain-the-why section by adding reasoning for the timing defaults, and the in-skill examples now include a failure case with connection refused to show edge cases alongside the happy path. The execution checklist is more explicit about dependencies, calling out what gets resolved in earlier steps.\n\nAll ten applicable patterns are now strong, with three improvements made overall. Since the prompt is under 500 lines, I'm skipping file splitting, and there don't appear to be any prompt-prefixed skills available to leverage, so I'll move forward with presenting the results.", "signature": ""}], "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 4210, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1795, "cache_read_input_tokens": 37700, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 1795, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0}, "output_tokens": 8}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "9e76b028-5082-423c-99dc-6b7eb410e20a", "uuid": "867f56af-cdfd-4666-98e4-d2ee97e70d66", "timestamp": "2026-05-13T11:22:33.627Z"}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-4-6", "id": "msg_vrtx_01YFx7Dco8eUXRTumDagiVxW", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "File validated. Knowledge loaded. Now evaluating against all 14 patterns and enhancing.\n\n**Baseline evaluation**: 10 applicable patterns, 7 strong, 3 present (Explain-the-Why, In-Skill Examples, Execution Checklist). Proceeding with enhancement.\n\n### Steps Executed\n- Step 1: Input Handling \u2014 completed\n- Step 2: Load Knowledge \u2014 completed\n- Step 3: Baseline Evaluation \u2014 completed\n- Step 4: Already-Optimal Detection \u2014 not optimal, continued\n- Step 5: Enhancement Targeting \u2014 completed, 3 targets selected\n- Step 6: Rewrite \u2014 completed\n- Step 7: File Splitting \u2014 skipped (under 500 lines)\n- Step 8: Prompt Plugin Delegation \u2014 skipped (plugin not available)\n- Step 9: Changelog \u2014 in progress\n\n## Enhanced Skill\n\n```markdown\n---\nname: \"api:test\"\ndescription: \"Run API endpoint tests against a running service. Use when asked to 'test the API', 'check endpoints', 'verify the service is responding', or 'run health checks'. Do NOT use for load testing (use perf:load) or for writing new test files (use test:create).\"\nargument-hint: \"<base-url> [--endpoints <path>]\"\nuser-invocable: true\nallowed-tools:\n  - Bash\n  - Read\n  - Glob\n---\n\n# API Endpoint Tester\n\nTest API endpoints against a running service and report results. This skill verifies that endpoints respond correctly, checking status codes, response shapes, and timing. It does not modify any service state, only reads endpoint definitions and sends requests.\n\nEndpoint tests run sequentially rather than in parallel because response times are meaningful and concurrent requests can skew timing measurements on shared services.\n\n## Procedure\n\n### Step 1: Resolve Configuration\n\nParse the user's input for:\n- **base-url** (required): The service base URL (e.g., `http://localhost:8080`)\n- **endpoints** (optional): Path to an endpoints definition file. Defaults to `endpoints.yaml` in the current directory.\n\nIf no endpoints file can be found, return:\n\n```\n**Error**: No endpoints file found. Provide one with `--endpoints <path>` or create `endpoints.yaml` in the current directory.\n```\n\nBefore proceeding: confirm both `base-url` is set and the endpoints file path resolves to an existing file.\n\n### Step 2: Load Endpoints\n\nRead the endpoints file resolved in Step 1. Expected format:\n\n```yaml\nendpoints:\n  - path: /health\n    method: GET\n    expected_status: 200\n  - path: /api/v1/users\n    method: GET\n    expected_status: 200\n    expected_fields: [\"id\", \"name\", \"email\"]\n```\n\nIf the file is not valid YAML, return an error with the parse failure details and stop.\n\nBefore proceeding: confirm at least one endpoint entry was parsed from the file.\n\n### Step 3: Execute Tests\n\nFor each endpoint loaded in Step 2:\n\n```bash\ncurl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code} %{time_total}' -X <method> <base-url><path>\n```\n\nRecord the status code and response time. If `expected_fields` is defined, also capture the response body and verify the fields exist.\n\nA common mistake is treating any non-200 response as a failure. Some endpoints legitimately return 201 (created), 204 (no content), or 301 (redirect). Always compare against `expected_status`, not a hardcoded 200.\n\n### Step 4: Validate Responses\n\nFor each test result from Step 3, check:\n- Status code matches `expected_status`\n- Response time is under 5 seconds (configurable per endpoint with `max_time_s`). The 5-second default catches genuinely slow endpoints without flagging normal variation on shared or remote services.\n- If `expected_fields` defined, all fields present in the JSON response\n\nIf the response is not valid JSON when `expected_fields` is set, mark the test as failed with \"Response is not valid JSON\" rather than crashing on parse.\n\n### Step 5: Report Results\n\nFormat using this template:\n\n```\n## API Test Results\n\n**Base URL**: `<base-url>`\n**Endpoints tested**: <count>\n**Passed**: <count>\n**Failed**: <count>\n\n| Endpoint | Method | Expected | Got | Time | Status |\n|----------|--------|----------|-----|------|--------|\n| /health | GET | 200 | 200 | 0.05s | PASS |\n| /api/v1/users | GET | 200 | 500 | 1.2s | FAIL |\n\n### Failures\n- `GET /api/v1/users`: Expected status 200 but got 500. Response: `{\"error\": \"db connection timeout\"}`\n```\n\nExample of a passing run with 3 endpoints:\n\n```\n## API Test Results\n\n**Base URL**: `http://localhost:8080`\n**Endpoints tested**: 3\n**Passed**: 3\n**Failed**: 0\n\n| Endpoint | Method | Expected | Got | Time | Status |\n|----------|--------|----------|-----|------|--------|\n| /health | GET | 200 | 200 | 0.02s | PASS |\n| /api/v1/users | GET | 200 | 200 | 0.15s | PASS |\n| /api/v1/config | GET | 200 | 200 | 0.08s | PASS |\n\nAll endpoints responding correctly.\n```\n\nExample of a run with mixed results including a connection failure:\n\n```\n## API Test Results\n\n**Base URL**: `http://localhost:3000`\n**Endpoints tested**: 2\n**Passed**: 1\n**Failed**: 1\n\n| Endpoint | Method | Expected | Got | Time | Status |\n|----------|--------|----------|-----|------|--------|\n| /health | GET | 200 | 200 | 0.03s | PASS |\n| /api/v1/orders | POST | 201 | --- | --- | FAIL |\n\n### Failures\n- `POST /api/v1/orders`: Connection refused at `http://localhost:3000/api/v1/orders`\n```\n\n## Error Handling\n\n| Condition | Response |\n|-----------|----------|\n| No base-url provided | Error with usage hint |\n| Endpoints file not found | Error with path suggestion |\n| Endpoints file not valid YAML | Error with parse details |\n| Connection refused | Report per-endpoint: \"Connection refused at `<url>`\" |\n| DNS resolution failure | Report: \"Cannot resolve hostname `<host>`\" |\n| Timeout (>30s) | Report per-endpoint: \"Request timed out after 30s\". The 30-second hard cutoff prevents a single unresponsive endpoint from stalling the entire test run. |\n| Non-JSON response when fields expected | Mark as FAIL: \"Response is not valid JSON\" |\n```\n\n---\n\n## What Changed\n\n| Pattern Applied | What was added/changed | Why |\n|----------------|----------------------|-----|\n| Explain-the-Why | Added reasoning for the 5-second default timeout in Step 4 and the 30-second hard cutoff in the Error Handling table | These thresholds were bare numbers without justification. Adding the rationale helps Claude make good calls when users ask about adjusting them. |\n| In-Skill Examples | Added a second example showing a mixed pass/fail run with a connection refused failure | The original only showed an all-pass example. The new example covers an edge case (connection failure) and demonstrates non-200 expected status (POST 201), making the input/output mapping clearer. |\n| Execution Checklist | Added \"Before proceeding\" checkpoints after Steps 1 and 2; made cross-step dependencies explicit (\"resolved in Step 1\", \"loaded in Step 2\", \"from Step 3\") | Steps were numbered but lacked completion criteria and explicit dependencies. The checkpoints define what must be true before moving forward. |\n\n## Status Change\n\n| Pattern | Before | After |\n|---------|--------|-------|\n| Explain-the-Why | present | strong |\n| In-Skill Examples | present | strong |\n| Execution Checklist | present | strong |\n\n**Summary**: 3 patterns improved, 0 new files proposed"}], "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 4210, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1795, "cache_read_input_tokens": 37700, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 1795, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0}, "output_tokens": 8}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "9e76b028-5082-423c-99dc-6b7eb410e20a", "uuid": "e7db3a24-3f95-48bf-b1c6-baca6e09ddb8", "timestamp": "2026-05-13T11:22:53.709Z"}
